<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ernest de Sarzec</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1832–1901</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ernest de Sarzec was a French diplomat and archaeologist who, as vice-consul at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, excavated the mound of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Telloh</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 1877. His finds, including statues of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea-cylinders">Gudea Cylinders</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stele-of-the-vultures">Stele of the Vultures</a>, revealed the Sumerian civilisation of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> and are now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>. He continued his excavations there until 1900.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ernest-de-sarzec.jpg" alt="Ernest de Sarzec" width="320" height="434" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ernest_de_Sarzec_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
